  - m_Id: 13410486567211013
    m_Localized: Ban Jalae 2
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211014
    m_Localized: '<b>History</b>  Interpreted as an early stupa at ancient Yarang,
      later adapted into a worship hall through rebuilding and enlargement.


      <b>Structure</b> 
      A front hall leads to an inner room, possibly for a sacred image. Later brickwork
      imitated timber walls.


      <b>Finds</b>  Excavations recovered stupa fragments,
      glass beads, gold-sheet fragments and earring moulds.


      <b>Restored model</b> 
      The roof and missing upper details are interpretations of a possible complete
      building.


      <size=26>Sources: Ancient Yarang, p. 165;

      Archaeology
      & History of the Three Southern Border Provinces, p. 65.</size>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211015
    m_Localized: Ban Jalae 3
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211016
    m_Localized: '<b>History</b>  A Buddhist brick monument built in two phases.
      Letter forms on votive tablets suggest an early phase around the 7th century
      CE.


      <b>Structure</b>  Later building enlarged the base and covered
      the earlier stairs. The surviving base measures about 13.5 by 13.6 metres.


      <b>Finds</b> 
      Numerous votive tablets and miniature stupa fragments were found in the central
      chamber and on offering platforms.


      <b>Restored model</b>  The missing
      upper structure is an interpretation, rather than an exact replica.


      <size=26>Sources:
      Ancient Yarang, p. 180;

      Archaeology & History of the Three Southern
      Border Provinces, p. 66.</size>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211017
    m_Localized: Ban Jalae 8
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211018
    m_Localized: '<b>Role</b>  Interpreted as the principal shrine of the Ban Jalae
      religious group at ancient Yarang. It faces west.


      <b>Structure</b> 
      A 14-metre-square brick base supports a central chamber. Sandstone corner-column
      bases suggest timber columns; a hall was added in front.


      <b>Finds</b> 
      Discoveries include a bronze Avalokiteshvara head, a sandstone bull sculpture
      and miniature stupa fragments.


      <b>Restored model</b>  The missing upper
      form, colour and decoration remain interpretations.


      <size=26>Sources:
      Ancient Yarang, p. 195;

      Archaeology & History of the Three Southern
      Border Provinces, pp. 64, 68.</size>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211019
    m_Localized: Ban Wat 9
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13410486567211020
    m_Localized: '<b>Role</b>  Interpreted as a Buddhist monument in the Ban Wat
      group at ancient Yarang.


      <b>Structure</b>  Excavation revealed a nearly
      square brick base, about 12.17 by 12.25 metres, with eastern and western stairs.
      Brick partitions and paved rooms survive above the base.


      <b>Finds</b> 
      Discoveries include a stucco elephant, pottery fragments, clay spouts, gold
      and glass beads.


      <b>Restored model</b>  The upper building and its
      decoration visualize a possible form above the surviving ruins.


      <size=26>Sources:
      Ancient Yarang, p. 208;

      Archaeology & History of the Three Southern
      Border Provinces, p. 62.</size>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 13458397711732736
    m_Localized: MAIN ATTRACTION
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
